    {
      "filename": "American University President's House.jpg",
      "title": "File:American University President's House.jpg",
      "url": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/90/American_University_President%27s_House.jpg",
      "descriptionUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:American_University_President%27s_House.jpg",
      "width": 2289,
      "height": 1886,
      "mime": "image/jpeg",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "licenseUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0",
      "artist": "American University",
      "description": "\"This house will have one of the most superb locations to be found in the District of\nColumbia. On a clear day, it will have a view of nearly 25 miles to the Blue Ridge Mountains.\"\n(The American University Courier, December 1924.)\nCompleted in 1925, this building served as the residence for the\nuniversity's Chancellors, later called Presidents. The exterior is a\nreproduction of the Macey House on Nantucket Island built in 1854.\nThe President's Building stands in what was the front yard of the\nGeneral Murdock Home -- \"The Old Farmhouse\" -- which originally\nstood on this property. The first Chancellor to live in this house was\nLucius C. Clark (1922-1932). In 1958, university President Hurst R.\nAnderson (1952-1968) moved to a private residence and the building\n\nwas converted into administrative offices.",
      "dateCreated": "2018-03-25 17:05:01",
      "sourceType": "commons-category",
      "sourceArticleId": "american-university-wp",
      "sourceGeohash4": "dqcj"
    },
